    "body": "How do _recursive_ descendant navigables/documents get destroyed after this change? All of the \"and its descendants\" algorithms seem to only operate on their children that are [one-level deep](https://whatpr.org/html/9907/41c1f94...a49f99e/document-lifecycle.html#unloading-documents:child-navigable), and run the \"single\" variant algorithms directly on them, which don't go deeper (I think). Contrast this (I think) with the [this](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/C#updating-the-traversable:descendant-navigables) and [this](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#destroying-documents:descendant-navigables) in the current spec — usages of `#descendant-navigables`. For example, if you trace the new [`#close-[...]`](https://whatpr.org/html/9907/41c1f94...a49f99e/document-sequences.html#close-a-top-level-traversable), I think you find that recursive descendants never get properly unloaded / destroyed / aborted, but I could be wrong? Maybe we're relying on https://whatpr.org/html/9907/41c1f94...a49f99e/browsing-the-web.html#document-state:destroy-a-document-and-its-descendants getting triggered, because recursive descendants are no longer considered \"fully active\" (I think?? Actually I'm not sure) if their ancestors are gone? That doesn't seem robust though...",
