    "body": "This runs `afterAllUnloads` on the document's main thread, right? In that case, it looks like we're [running \"destroy a top-level traversable\"](https://whatpr.org/html/9907/41c1f94...a49f99e/document-sequences.html#garbage-collection-and-browsing-contexts:destroy-a-top-level-traversable-2) on the main thread, which calls [`#destroy-a-document-and-its-descendants`](https://whatpr.org/html/9907/41c1f94...a49f99e/document-lifecycle.html#destroy-a-document-and-its-descendants), which synchronously \"waits\" for its immediate descendant frames to report back that they've been destroyed and aborted successfully on _their_ main threads. All of this blocks the \"top-level\" document's main thread on these cross-process document destruction/aborts, which seems bad right? I don't think implementations do this.",
