    "body": "> I believe that specifically for the close case (which supplies afterAllUnloads), we delay destroying until all descendants have had a chance to fire their unload events.\r\n\r\nI mean it's true that _afterAllUnloads_ runs, indeed all descendants have had a chance to fire their unload events, but I think that's not exactly what I'm commenting on. I'm commenting on the fact _afterAllUnloads_ runs on the top-level document's main thread, which means on the document's main thread, we call:\r\n\r\n 1. https://whatpr.org/html/9907/41c1f94...a49f99e/document-sequences.html#destroy-a-top-level-traversable, which calls (below)\r\n 2. https://whatpr.org/html/9907/41c1f94...a49f99e/document-lifecycle.html#destroy-a-document-and-its-descendants\r\n\r\nThese algorithms, running on the main thread, queue a bunch of parallel tasks to descendant-document-main-threads, and \"Wait\" for them to finish. This blocks _at least_ the top-level document's main thread on a bunch of cross-document/process tasks reporting back successful destruction. But in implementations, I feel like this is done in parallel. I don't think Chromium ever blocks the top-level main thread's event loop / script execution on the parallel destruction of arbitrary descendants. Does that make sense?",
